      "id": "s9rqhk1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. Acid–Base Theories"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Acids release H+ (hydronium) ions in aqueous solutions and turn blue litmus paper red.",
        "Bases produce hydroxide (OH-) ions in water, taste bitter, and turn red litmus paper blue.",
        "The pH scale quantifies acidity and alkalinity: pH < 7 is acidic, pH = 7 is neutral, and pH > 7 is basic.",
        "Acid rain occurs when atmospheric pollutants like SO2 and NO2 lower rainwater pH below 5.6.",
        "Water of crystallisation refers to the fixed number of water molecules present in one formula unit of a salt crystal."
      ],
      "id": "b4eau6h"
    },
